  <div class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between a visa and a residence permit?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>A visa is permission to enter Spain, while a residence permit is documentation that allows you to legally live in Spain. Many people begin with a visa (such as a Non-Lucrative or Digital Nomad visa) and then transition to a residence permit once they establish residency.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to get a Spanish residence permit?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Processing times vary, but typically 2-4 months from submission. This can be longer during peak season or if additional documentation is required. We recommend submitting applications as early as possible.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I work on a residence permit?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Temporary residence permits typically allow work authorization. Permanent residence permits grant full employment rights. Always verify your specific permit conditions, as some permits (like certain study permits) may have restrictions.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is a TIE card?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The TIE (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero) is Spain's official foreigner identification card. Once your residence permit is approved, you apply for a TIE card, which serves as your primary identification document in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need a residence permit if I have a non-lucrative visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>A Non-Lucrative visa allows you to reside in Spain, but it's technically a visa rather than a residence permit. Many expats eventually transition to a residence permit for additional benefits and clarity, though this isn't always mandatory.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much does a Spanish residence permit cost?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Residence permit application fees are typically €0-150 depending on the type. TIE card costs vary but are usually €20-100. Additional costs include notarization, translation, and professional assistance if using legal support.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What documents do I need to apply for a residence permit?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Required documents typically include your passport, proof of economic means (bank statements, employment contract), health insurance, proof of address, criminal records clearance, and visa documentation. Requirements vary based on permit type. Consult our detailed requirements section or book a consultation.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I renew my residence permit?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Residence permits are renewable. Temporary permits last 1 year (renewable for up to 5 years), while permanent permits can be renewed every 5 years. Renewal processes typically begin 60 days before expiry.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
